Pins resolve with this precedence, and each is reported with its source (`project`, `gpux-checkout` or `default`):

1. the project: the `wasm-bindgen` version from its `Cargo.lock`; the stable toolchain from `rust-toolchain.toml`; and `[workspace.metadata.rayx]` keys `web-toolchain`, `node`, `jdk`, `playwright`, `android-ndk`, `android-platform`, `android-build-tools`, `android-ndk-api`, `android-system-image`, `xcode`;
2. for a gpux checkout (a workspace containing the package `gpux-testkit`) that declares nothing: gpux's pins — platform `android-36`, build-tools `36.0.0`, NDK `28.0.12674087`;
3. otherwise the built-in defaults: nightly `nightly-2026-06-23`, Node 22, JDK 21, Playwright `1.59.1`, NDK `28.0.12674087`, platform `android-34`, cargo-ndk API 31, no build-tools pin, the newest stable Xcode.

The nightly and JDK defaults are the same in all three layers. Outside any project only the defaults apply. Tested with fixture workspaces with and without metadata and lockfiles, and with a gpux-checkout fixture.

**Base set on Windows x64 and ARM64** through winget:

- Visual Studio 2022 Build Tools with the `Microsoft.VisualStudio.Workload.VCTools` workload and a Windows SDK, plus `Microsoft.VisualStudio.Component.VC.Tools.ARM64` on ARM64;
- the newest Windows SDK `fxc.exe` directory on the user PATH (the `arm64` bin directory on ARM64 hosts, `x64` otherwise);
- LLVM/Clang (`LLVM.LLVM`) with `C:\Program Files\LLVM\bin` on PATH — required on ARM64, where `ring` needs Clang for its assembly;
- VC++ redistributables: `Microsoft.VCRedist.2015+.arm64` and `.x64` on ARM64, `.x64` on x64;
- rustup with the native host triple (`aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` on ARM64, never the emulated x64 one), the project's toolchain, and `%USERPROFILE%\.cargo\bin` on PATH.

Missing winget fails with the App Installer instruction.

**`--android`:** everything RayX xtask's `setup-android` ensures today, plus a complete Android workstation:

- the JDK at the pinned major, 21 by default (Microsoft OpenJDK through winget or Homebrew, `openjdk-<major>-jdk` on Ubuntu), with `JAVA_HOME` set for the user;
- **Android Studio** on desktop hosts: `Google.AndroidStudio` through winget, `android-studio` cask on macOS, `sudo snap install android-studio --classic` on Ubuntu desktops; inside WSL it is skipped and reported as provided by the Windows host;
- the Android SDK, found the way xtask finds it (`ANDROID_HOME`, `ANDROID_SDK_ROOT`, then the platform default locations, which Android Studio also uses); when none exists, the command-line tools are downloaded from Google into the default location and `ANDROID_HOME` is set for the user;
- `sdkmanager` packages `platform-tools`, `platforms;<platform>`, `ndk;<ndk>` (unless an NDK is already found through `ANDROID_NDK_HOME`, `ANDROID_NDK_ROOT`, `NDK_HOME` or `<sdk>/ndk/*`, as xtask does), `build-tools;<build-tools>` when pinned, `emulator`, and a system image for the host CPU (`x86_64` or `arm64-v8a`) at the pinned platform;
- a default AVD `rayx-<platform>-<abi>` created with `avdmanager` when no AVD exists, so `rayx app ... run android` can start one autonomously;
- emulator acceleration: `/dev/kvm` access on Linux (adding the user to the `kvm` group through `sudo`), the Windows Hypervisor Platform feature on Windows (enabled through an elevated `dism` with a reboot notice), nothing extra on macOS;
- `cargo-ndk` (`cargo install cargo-ndk --locked`) and the Rust targets `aarch64-linux-android` and `x86_64-linux-android`.

SDK licenses are shown to the user by `sdkmanager --licenses`; only `--yes` accepts them non-interactively.

`rayx doctor` prints, for every requirement set that applies to the host, each requirement's status (present with version, missing, wrong version) and the exact command that fixes it, plus environment findings:

- the GPU adapters with vendor, driver version and whether a hardware adapter is usable (`hardwareAdapter`);
- whether this is an interactive desktop session that can open headed browsers and windows (`interactiveDesktop`: a console session on Windows, `DISPLAY`/`WAYLAND_DISPLAY` on Linux, a GUI login on macOS);
- WSL PATH order (`pnpm`, `wasm-bindgen`, `cargo` resolving to `/mnt/c`), `VK_DRIVER_FILES` and lavapipe under WSL, and the WSL disk size when run on Windows;
- the pins in use with their source.

`--json` prints the same as one JSON document with `requirementCount`, `hardwareAdapter` and `interactiveDesktop`. Doctor exits 0 whenever it could inspect the host; `setup --check` is the command that fails on missing requirements.

`rayx setup --wsl [set flags]` on Windows:

- requires WSL 2 (`wsl --version`), and installs `Ubuntu-24.04` with `wsl --install -d Ubuntu-24.04` when absent;
- when the distribution has no default user yet, stops and tells the user to open Ubuntu once and create the user, then re-run;
- installs the Linux `rayx` of the same version inside the distribution (the matching static musl release asset; `RAYX_WSL_BINARY=<path>` overrides it for development builds), into `~/.local/bin`;
- runs `rayx setup` with the same flags inside the distribution in the same console, so `sudo` prompts reach the user.

On other hosts `--wsl` fails with a message. Planning is tested with a fake runner; the end-to-end run is verified manually on Windows.

`rayx wsl compact [--distro <name>] [--clean] [--yes]` on Windows gives unused WSL disk space back to Windows:

1. with `--clean`, lists the build-output directories under the cloned checkouts with their sizes and deletes them after confirmation (or at once with `--yes`);
2. runs `sudo fstrim -av` inside the distribution in the user's console;
3. warns that `wsl --shutdown` stops every distribution, including VS Code remote sessions, and continues after confirmation (or at once with `--yes`);
4. runs `wsl --shutdown`, then compacts the virtual disk in one elevated child process: `Optimize-VHD -Path <vhdx> -Mode Full` when the Hyper-V module is available, otherwise a `diskpart` script (`select vdisk`, `attach vdisk readonly`, `compact vdisk`, `detach vdisk`);
5. reports the disk size before and after.

It never enables sparse disks (`wsl --manage --set-sparse`), which WSL 2.6 disabled because they can corrupt the distribution. Inside WSL the command prints the Windows command to run instead. The step plan is tested with fakes; the real compaction is verified manually on this machine, with the before/after sizes recorded.

`rayx app [<dir>] <action> <target> [flags]` accepts exactly RayX xtask's grammar today:

- actions `build`, `run`, `pack`, `publish` (staging only, as now), `deploy` (Android) and `test` (WASM), plus `rayx app [<dir>] assets generate|validate|list`;
- targets `host`, `windows`, `linux`, `macos`, `mobile`, `ios`, `android`, `wasm` (alias `web`);
- release by default, `--debug`/`--development`, `--release` rejected; `--features`, `--all-features`, `--no-default-features` and `--` pass-through;
- every Android flag (`--android-device`, `--android-connect`, `--android-paired`, `--android-wireless`, `--android-apk`, the DevKit flags, the native and Gradle size flags, `--size-optimized`, `--no-launch`), the iOS `--simulator`/`--device`, and the WASM `--port`, `--headed`, `--webgpu` and `--playwright-test`;
- the `rayx_diagnostics` policy: only diagnostic-harness apps may use it, and never for pack, publish or deploy.

`<dir>` defaults to the current directory. The xtask tests of these modules move with the code and pass; the ones that read RayX's `apps/lab`, `apps/examples_mobile` or `crates/` are rewritten against fixture apps in this repository, and staging output goes to temporary directories instead of a repository's `artifacts-temp`.

The pipeline is project-agnostic:

- the app directory resolves against the current directory, and the workspace root and packages come from `cargo metadata` of the selected app (PIN-1); `artifacts/`, `artifacts-temp/` and `target/` are under that workspace root for every target, including desktop `pack`;
- the generated Android, iOS and WASM entry crates depend on `rayx` (and `rayx_devkit` when the `devkit` feature is selected) with the **same source the app itself uses** as `cargo metadata` reports it (path made absolute, git with its revision, or registry version), never a fixed `crates/rayx` path;
- `[patch]` entries are copied from the selected workspace's root manifest when the app needs them, as xtask does for `indxdb`/`rexie`;
- the gpux font assets come from the `gpux-fonts` package's manifest directory as `cargo metadata` resolves it, not from a sibling checkout path;
- the threaded build uses the pinned nightly and the project's locked `wasm-bindgen` (PIN-2) in generated manifests, and fails with `rayx setup --web` as the fix when the `wasm-bindgen` CLI on PATH has another version;
- desktop target triples follow the native host architecture (`aarch64-pc-windows-msvc` on Windows ARM64);
- no re-exec through `cargo run`: `rayx` is already a release binary.

Tested with fixture workspaces outside the RayX layout, including a registry-style `rayx` and `gpux-fonts` source.

On the real RayX checkout, with `rayx` built from this repository and RayX as the current directory, these succeed and write the same artifact layout as xtask (`artifacts/apps/<slug>/wasm/<profile>/` with `index.html`, the wasm-bindgen output and `assets/`; the APK under `artifacts/apps/<slug>/android/`):

- `rayx app apps/lab build wasm`;
- `rayx app apps/test_suite build wasm --no-default-features --features rayx_diagnostics`;
- `rayx app apps/lab build host`;
- `rayx app apps/examples_mobile build android` with JDK 21;
- the focused headed WebGPU spec `rayx app apps/test_suite test wasm --headed --webgpu --no-default-features --features rayx_diagnostics --playwright-test test_suite/rayx-collapsible-sizes.spec.ts`, after `rayx doctor` reports a hardware adapter and an interactive desktop.

The threaded-WASM toolchain is `nightly-2026-06-23` in every place that names one:

- RayX.CLI's default (PIN-2);
- RayX: `[workspace.metadata.rayx] web-toolchain = "nightly-2026-06-23"` in the root `Cargo.toml`, xtask's WASM build until sprint 7 removes it, CI installing that toolchain with `rust-src` and the wasm32 target, `README.md` and the RayX skill's `diagnostics.md`;
- Gpux: gpux-testkit's default toolchain (`GPUX_WEB_TOOLCHAIN` still overrides it) and its test, `docs/testing.md`, `docs/installation-and-usage.md`, `LINUX-WSL.md`, and the gpux skill references `installation-and-usage.md` and `multithreaded-wasm-apps.md`; `lane-b.yml` already pins it.

A `git grep -P` in RayX and in Gpux (outside `.agentx/`) finds no unpinned `+nightly`, `toolchain install nightly`, `--toolchain nightly` or `"nightly"` default; `cargo test -p gpux-testkit --test web` passes.

A new RayX skill reference `.claude/skills/rayx/references/wasm-toolchain-update.md`, linked from the skill's `SKILL.md`, tells an agent how to move the threaded-WASM nightly:

- how to find a newer nightly worth taking (the current pin's date, `rustup toolchain install nightly-<date>`, what to read in the Rust release notes for `build-std`, atomics and `wasm32-unknown-unknown` changes);
- how to prove it: the threaded builds of Lab and the Test Suite through `rayx`, gpux's `gpux-testkit web build --verify`, and one focused headed WebGPU spec;
- **every place to update together**, with the `git grep` that finds them: RayX.CLI's default in `project::pins` (and its tests), RayX's root `[workspace.metadata.rayx] web-toolchain` and CI, Gpux's testkit default, `lane-b.yml` `GPUX_WEB_TOOLCHAIN` and the docs from PINX-1;
- that the three repositories change in paired commits.

The gpux skill's `multithreaded-wasm-apps.md` points to it in one line. Verified by review.

RayX moves every caller to `rayx`:

- CI (`.github/workflows/pr-test.yml`) installs `rayx` at a pinned version (the release installer once a release exists, before that `cargo install --locked --git https://github.com/rayx-ui/RayX.CLI --rev <commit>`) and calls `rayx app ...` and `rayx setup --test` where it called xtask or ran the `setup-wasm` steps by hand;
- `docs/src/xtask.md` and `docs/src/xtask/*.md` become the `rayx` guide, and the other pages that show xtask commands (theming, text selection, text system, actions/input maps) use `rayx`;
- `README.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, the app READMEs (`apps/lab`, `apps/examples_mobile`, `apps/test_suite`) and the RayX skill name `rayx` commands;
- the `tools-node` scripts that run `cargo run --release -p xtask` (`test_suite/android-pointer-gesture-probe.mjs`, `test_suite/rayx-components-base-completion-targets.mjs`) run `rayx`.

A `git grep` for `xtask -- app`, `-p xtask -- app`, `setup-wasm` and `setup-android` outside `.agentx/` returns nothing. RayX memory under `.agentx/` is updated during decision promotion at epic end, not by this criterion.
